; MNIST by hand (Stage 4).
;
; Write the whole MNIST program yourself, one instruction per line, using the numbers
; below. The test assembles this file, drops the instructions into the compiled memory
; image in place of the compiler's own, and runs it on the simulator. If the ten
; results match, you have written the same program the compiler writes in Stage 3.
;
; Read docs/04-mnist-by-hand.md first. The short version:
;
;   memory=   a BYTE address in main memory (where the compiler put something)
;   index=    an ELEMENT index inside a scratchpad (not a byte address)
;   count=    how many ELEMENTS to move, never how many bytes
;
; Elements are one byte in ACTIVATION_SCRATCHPAD and WEIGHT_SCRATCHPAD and four bytes
; in BIAS_SCRATCHPAD and ACCUMULATORS. So count=128 into BIAS_SCRATCHPAD reads 512
; bytes of main memory, but count is still 128.
;
; Main memory layout (printed by `python -m cub compile`):
;   weights1   0x00400   8-bit,  128 x 784, one row per output
;   biases1    0x18C00   32-bit, 128 values
;   weights2   0x18E00   8-bit,  10 x 128
;   biases2    0x19300   32-bit, 10 values
;   input      0x19340   8-bit,  784 values   (the host writes the image here)
;   output     0x19680   32-bit, 10 values    (your last STORE writes the answer here)
;
; Scratchpad plan (yours to follow; the compiler uses the same one):
;   ACTIVATION_SCRATCHPAD: input image at index 0, hidden activations at index 1024
;   WEIGHT_SCRATCHPAD:     weights1 at index 0, weights2 right after it
;   BIAS_SCRATCHPAD:       biases1 at index 0, biases2 right after it
;   ACCUMULATORS:          always index 0
;   layer-1 shift:         12

; ---- layer 1: 784 inputs -> 128 outputs, then rectified linear ----
LOAD             space=ACTIVATION_SCRATCHPAD  memory=0x19340  index=0  count=784
; --- SOLUTION(stage=4): load weights1 into WEIGHT_SCRATCHPAD index 0 (count = 128*784) and biases1 into BIAS_SCRATCHPAD index 0 (count = 128); MATRIX_MULTIPLY the image at input=0 by those weights into accumulator=0 with outputs=128 inputs=784; ADD_BIAS those 128 accumulators; RECTIFIED_LINEAR them into ACTIVATION_SCRATCHPAD destination=1024 with shift=12 ---
LOAD             space=WEIGHT_SCRATCHPAD  memory=0x400    index=0  count=100352
LOAD             space=BIAS_SCRATCHPAD    memory=0x18C00  index=0  count=128
MATRIX_MULTIPLY  input=0  weights=0  accumulator=0  outputs=128  inputs=784
ADD_BIAS         accumulator=0  bias=0  count=128
RECTIFIED_LINEAR accumulator=0  destination=1024  count=128  shift=12
; --- END SOLUTION ---

; ---- layer 2: 128 inputs -> 10 outputs, and those ten numbers are the answer ----
; --- SOLUTION(stage=4): load weights2 and biases2 into the scratchpads *after* layer 1's, so their index= operands are 100352 and 128, not 0; MATRIX_MULTIPLY from input=1024 (the hidden activations) with outputs=10 inputs=128; ADD_BIAS; then STORE 10 elements from ACCUMULATORS index 0 to memory=0x19680 instead of a RECTIFIED_LINEAR, because the last layer's raw 32-bit accumulators are the answer ---
LOAD             space=WEIGHT_SCRATCHPAD  memory=0x18E00  index=100352  count=1280
LOAD             space=BIAS_SCRATCHPAD    memory=0x19300  index=128     count=10
MATRIX_MULTIPLY  input=1024  weights=100352  accumulator=0  outputs=10  inputs=128
ADD_BIAS         accumulator=0  bias=128  count=10
STORE            space=ACCUMULATORS  memory=0x19680  index=0  count=10
; --- END SOLUTION ---
HALT
